 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Overview",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "Eight pins, one supply. VM powers both the control circuits and the motor; an internal charge pump drives the high-side N-channel gates above VM. The bridge's on resistance is 565 mΩ typ counting the pair of FETs in the current path, upper plus lower. PWM at 0 to 100 kHz sets the speed. Both inputs low gives sleep, and the protection features keep a system fault from damaging the part."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Bridge control and PWM",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "Inputs held at fixed levels give 100% duty drive; PWM on them gives variable speed. Alternating drive with brake usually works best: for forward at half speed, IN1 stays at 1 and IN2 toggles between 0 (drive) and 1 (brake). Alternating drive with coast (both 0) gives fast decay instead. IN1 and IN2 may be driven before VM is present."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Sleep",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "Both inputs low for tSLEEP (1 ms typ) puts the outputs in high impedance and the supply current down to IVMSLEEP. Powering up with both inputs low goes straight to sleep. Once IN1 or IN2 has been high for 5 µs or more, the part is running 50 µs (tON) later."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Current regulation",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "The current through the sense resistor on ISEN is compared with VREF scaled by AV. On reaching ITRIP the part turns on both low-side FETs for tOFF (25 µs typ), a slow-decay period, and then restores the state the inputs ask for. The following drive time until the next trip depends on VM, the motor's back-EMF and its inductance. A blanking time tBLANK (2 µs typ) follows each turn-on."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Dead time",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "Each output change from high to low or low to high has a high-impedance gap, tDEAD, to stop shoot-through. During it the output sits one body-diode drop below ground if current leaves the pin, or one diode drop above VM if current enters it."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Undervoltage lockout",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "VM below the UVLO threshold turns every bridge FET off; the bridge comes back once VM rises above the threshold."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Overcurrent protection",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "Output current above IOCP for longer than tOCP turns every bridge FET off for tRETRY (3 ms typ). The bridge then follows IN1 and IN2 again; a short that is still there repeats the cycle."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Thermal shutdown",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "Too hot a die turns every bridge FET off; operation restarts by itself when the die has cooled. The trip is about 175°C, and restart waits until the temperature has fallen by the hysteresis. A part that reaches TSD is dissipating too much power, is heatsunk too poorly or sits in too hot an ambient."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Modes of use",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "PWM with regulation: ITRIP sits above running current but high enough for a quick spin-up, one input is PWM, the other static, and brake is normally used in the off time. PWM without regulation: ISEN goes straight to the ground plane, VREF must still be 0.3 V to 5 V (higher gives more noise margin), and peaks up to 3.6 A are available for a few hundred milliseconds depending on the board and ambient; beyond 3.6 A OCP or TSD may trip, turning the part off for about 3 ms. Static inputs with regulation: 100% duty drive with ITRIP limiting current, speed and torque. Motor speed can also be set by varying VM."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Motor voltage",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "Pick VM from the motor's rating and the speed wanted. For one duty cycle a higher VM spins a brushed motor faster and makes current in its windings change faster."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Drive current",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "Current runs through one high-side FET, the motor and one low-side FET. The part has carried 2 A RMS at 25°C on standard FR-4 boards; the true limit moves with board design, ambient and PWM frequency. Switching the inputs at 200 kHz instead of 20 kHz typically adds 20% to the heat."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Sense resistor",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "Use a surface-mount, low-inductance resistor with enough power rating, close to the driver. Its dissipation climbs fast with current, and its rating falls at high ambient, so allow margin when other parts heat the board and measure its temperature in the finished product. Several ordinary resistors in parallel from the sense node to ground share the current and heat and are a common substitute for one power resistor."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Bulk capacitance",
   "page": 14,
   "text": "More local bulk capacitance generally helps; its cost is money and board area. How much is needed depends on the peak motor current, the supply's own capacitance and current capability, the wiring inductance to the supply, the ripple allowed, the motor type and how it is braked. Supply wiring inductance limits how fast supply current can change, so too little bulk capacitance lets VM swing as the motor draws or returns current. The final value comes from system testing. Rate bulk capacitors above the operating voltage, since the motor can pump energy back into the supply."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Layout",
   "page": 15,
   "text": "Place the bulk capacitor to keep the high-current loop through the driver short, with wide copper and many vias between layers. Small capacitors are ceramic and sit right at the pins. The outputs need wide traces. Solder the thermal pad to a top-layer ground plane and stitch it with many vias to a large bottom-layer ground plane to shed the I² × RDS(on) heat."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Power dissipation and heatsinking",
   "page": 15,
   "text": "Losses are dominated by the output FETs' on resistance. Start-up current is much higher than running current, and that peak and how long it lasts count too. RDS(on) rises as the part heats, so dissipation grows with temperature. The exposed pad must be joined thermally to board copper: on a board with a ground plane, by vias to it; on one without inner planes, by copper area on either side, with thermal vias when the copper is on the far side. More copper lets more power go."
  }
 ],
